A||||9||JohnnyK||Front upper a-arm spacers. How many??||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-20-2000||03:35 PM||||I think I might have discovered my sluggish steering problem, but I need<BR>someone to look behind there wheel for me (I'll owe ya)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> In between<BR>the front upper a-arm, I have 1 spacer (there are washer type things to<BR>the outside, but I mean to the inside) and then there is an extra .5" of<BR>space where the a-arm can slide back and forth.  I only had 1 spacer on<BR>the inside of each a-arm, but it looks like there should be two? I<BR>thought the play might have gone away when I hooked up the ball joints,<BR>but it is still there.  So, is there supposed to be two of the spacers?<BR>One on the inside of each side of the arm? Thanks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||07-20-2000||03:50 PM||||I just disassembled and replaced all of the rubber, ball joints, and tie rods (and a new rack) in my 86 SE.<P>The upper arms have two knurled washers on the inside betweenn the control arm and frame mount. They are two different thicknesses. Depending on which one is in the front or back you get +1 or -1 degree geometry for alignment. Both washers are needed to secure the bushings to the frame.<P>There were also two flat washers on the outside on each end of the bolt. They don't affect alignment.<P>Hope that helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||07-20-2000||04:07 PM||||Thats what I thought, but it seems like to take up the room, you need both of the thick washers? Or is there supposed to be a tiny amount of movement? (Or do you take up the slack by really cranking on the bolt??) Anyways, what is the difference on the geometry you can achieve?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||07-20-2000||06:16 PM||||The bolt will squeeze the control arm bushings together against the frame mount and take up the space. <P>I guess it's conceivable that one washer could be used but the manual makes no claim to that affect so I'd take it in for an alignment and let them get the right washers in there. You can't guess and get it right.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||07-20-2000||07:50 PM||||Well, with one washer the a-arm slides back and forth! I think they confused that with worn bushings when I took it in for safety (replaced those too)||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-20-2000||11:38 PM||||And therein lie the problem. I didn't think you could get away with one washer.||||reg||14
Z||000006||JohnnyK||07-22-2000||12:25 PM||||I went to an alignment shop, and they say they don't use these, and just use these "U" shaped shims that they drop in, but it looks like they would flip upside down and fall out? Soooo, instead of spending $55 for the washers (what a rip!), think it would pass safety if I just used normal, hardened steel washers? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||07-22-2000||12:58 PM||||Are you trying to avoid an alignment? If you are missing washers your alignment is shot. <P>Why not get it aligned and have them install the shims? They will stay in.<P>Frankly, I don't want to encourage you to do anything less.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||07-22-2000||01:18 PM||||No. This is a car that has been sitting for quite a few years, and I've almost got it back into running condition, except for this.  I need it to pass safety before I take it into do an alignment or anything like that.||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||07-22-2000||05:23 PM||||I made my own washers out of thick brass bushings. Will they last? (Will they pass safety?)||||reg||1
